// expect-exit: 0
//
// THE HEADER THIS FILE DID NOT HAVE UNTIL 0.0.1. See
// `case1_no_failsafe.npk`'s header for why the absence mattered (TM-115).
//
// THIS FILE'S EXPECTATION IS THE ONE THAT DID NOT CHANGE, and that is worth a
// marker of its own rather than a silence. Before the compiler's DEF-5 landed
// it compiled, linked and ran at exit 0; after, it compiles, links and runs at
// exit 0. It is the CONTROL that says the new refusal does not misfire: the
// only difference between this file and `case1_no_failsafe.npk` is the
// declaration below, and that difference is now the difference between a file
// `npkc` accepts and a file `npkc` refuses -- where it used to be the
// difference between a file `llc` accepted and one it refused.
//
// Measured at pin `0dfddac`: `npkc` exit 0 with a `.ll` written, one
// `@main` and one `@npk_failsafe` defined in it, `llc` 0, `ld.lld` 0, run 0.
// The exit code is paired with the artefact every time, because an exit code
// on its own is what O-N11 looked like.
//
// ==================== AS WRITTEN ON 2026-09-03 ==============================
// THE CONTRAST, and the reason case 1 is evidence rather than an assertion.
// Character for character `case1_no_failsafe.npk`, plus a `failsafe` naming
// the four unconditional floor arms and the wildcard.
//
// All four steps exit 0 and the program runs. So the difference between a file
// `npkc` accepts and links, and a file `npkc` accepts and `llc` refuses, is
// exactly the presence of the declaration below -- and `npkc`'s exit code is 0
// for both.
mod:case2_failsafe_present;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    exit 0i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
